      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L06-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "6.3: One Solution or Two?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The symbol \\(\\sqrt{11}\\) represents the positive square root of 11. If we want to represent the negative square root, we write \\(\\text{-} \\sqrt{11}\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L06-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "6.3: One Solution or Two?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The equation \\(x^2 = 11\\) has two solutions, because \\(\\sqrt{11}^2=11\\), and also\\(\\left(\\text{-}\\sqrt{11}\\right)^2=11\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L06-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "6.3: One Solution or Two?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The equation \\(\\sqrt{x} = 11\\) only has one solution, namely 121.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L06-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "6.3: One Solution or Two?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The equation \\(\\sqrt{x} = \\sqrt{11}\\) only has one solution, namely 11.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L06-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "6.3: One Solution or Two?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The equation \\(\\sqrt{x} = \\text{-}11\\) doesn’t have any solutions, because the left side is positive and the right side is negative, which is impossible, because a positive number cannot equal a negative number.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
